NOTICE
======

mirror_base.py in this repository began as a script written by AChillVamp, and a recognizable part
of their work is still in it. Thanks are owed for the original — it did the hard part, which was
working out that a webcomic can be walked automatically at all, and doing it in a way that has kept
working across years of sites redesigning themselves.

AChillVamp's archive.org page: https://archive.org/details/@achillvamp


What is still theirs
--------------------

Measured against the earliest copy still on hand (AChillVamp's v3.3, 168 lines), the current file
has grown to 656 lines and 18 functions. The added material - the metadata sidecar, the append-only
.cbz packing, resume points, exit codes, headless operation - is new, as are update_comics.py and
adopt_comic.py, which did not exist in any form in the original.

But volume is the wrong measure, and the parts that remain are the distinctive ones:

  * The XPath candidate lists. 11 of the 12 original image paths and 10 of the 14 next-page paths
    are still present word for word. Both lists have roughly doubled since, but the original
    selection sits inside them intact. It is a list someone assembled by hand against real sites,
    and it is the single most substantial piece of the original still in use.

  * The wording. Four of the eight argparse strings are unchanged, including the program's own
    description and the help text for -m and --element_find_next_manual.

  * The structure. setup -> img_save -> next -> ele_get / next_ele_get, and the approach of trying
    a list of candidate XPaths until one matches, are AChillVamp's design. Later work extended
    those functions rather than replacing them.


License status
--------------

No license was attached to the original, and no license grant for it has been located. This
repository therefore ships without one: a derivative work cannot be licensed more freely than what
it derives from, and putting a license file here would claim a grant that nobody is in a position
to give.

In practice that means the code is published for reading, for personal use, and for anyone who
wants to run it on their own comics - but not with any formal permission to redistribute it, and
certainly not commercially.

If AChillVamp finds this and would like the code licensed, removed, credited differently, or simply
handed back, that request will be honoured. Likewise if anyone knows how to reach them.

Contributions made since the original are by ZornTaov, with parts written using Anthropic's Claude.
